view and Enter to inspect."},["SPAN",{"class":"diss-pointer-guide__cursor","data-pointer-guide-icon":"true","aria-hidden":"true"},"?"],["SPAN",{"class":"diss-pointer-guide__body"},["STRONG",{"data-pointer-guide-label":"true"},"Pointer mode ? Inspect anatomy"],["SPAN",{"data-pointer-guide-instruction":"true"},"Select a visible structure, or use Arrow keys to preview and Enter to inspect."]]],["DIV",{"class":"diss-canvas-layout","data-split":"false"},["DIV",{"class":"diss-canvas-frame","data-diss-fullscreen-stage":"true"},["DIV",{"id":"diss-fullscreen-dock","class":"diss-fullscreen-dock","role":"toolbar","aria-label":"Fullscreen specimen view and tools"},["BUTTON",{"type":"button","class":"diss-fullscreen-dock__exit","data-diss-fullscreen-exit":"true","aria-label":"Exit fullscreen specimen mode","aria-keyshortcuts":"Escape","title":"Exit fullscreen (Escape)"},"Exit fullscreen"],["BUTTON",{"type":"button","aria-label":"Fullscreen anatomical view: cycle dorsal, ventral, lateral, and internal"},"View: ventral"],["BUTTON",{"type":"button","class":"diss-advanced-only","aria-label":"Fullscreen illumination color: cycle neutral, warm, and cool"},"Light: neutral"],["LABEL",{},["SPAN",{},"Illumination 68%"],["INPUT",{"__playwright_value_":"68","type":"range","min":"20","max":"100","step":"2","aria-label":"Fullscreen illumination intensity","aria-valuetext":"68 percent, Balanced illumination","value":"68"}]],["BUTTON",{"type":"button","class":"diss-advanced-only","aria-label":"Fullscreen light direction: cycle overhead, left, right, and raking"},"Angle: overhead"],["BUTTON",{"type":"button","class":"diss-advanced-only","aria-label":"Tissue: after technique. Activate to show the tissue before the technique."},"Tissue: after"],["BUTTON",{"type":"button","class":"diss-advanced-only","aria-label":"Fullscreen animation speed: cycle normal, slow, and fast"},"Speed: normal"],["LABEL",{},["SPAN",{},"Tool"],["SELECT",{"aria-label":"Fullscreen active instrument"},["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"true","value":"probe"},"Probe"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"forceps"},"Forceps"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"scalpel"},"Scalpel"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"scissors"},"Scissors"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"pin"},"Pins"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"dropper"},"Dropper"],["OPTION",{"__playwright_selected_":"false","value":"wick"},"Absorbent Wick"]]],["SPAN",{"class":"diss-fullscreen-dock__handoff","data-tone":"ready","aria-label":"Fullscreen next action: Find and inspect Pericardium. Identify"},"Next: Find and inspect Pericardium · Identify"]],["SPAN",{"id":"diss-canvas-label","class":"diss-sr-only"},"Sheep Heart interactive specimen · External layer, ventral view"],["SECTION",{"id":"diss-canvas-detail","class":"diss-sr-only","aria-labelledby":"diss-canvas-detail-heading"},["H4",{"id":"diss-canvas-detail-heading"},"Specimen canvas description"],["P",{},"Every canvas selection and drag action has a keyboard or button alternative. Use the structure directory for equivalent anatomical descriptions, the technique action button instead of drawing a stroke, the pin action button instead of dragging a pin, the probe action button instead of tracing a structure, and the pan controls instead of dragging a zoomed view. When the depth atlas is enabled, surface landmarks use circles, mid-depth landmarks use diamonds, and deep landmarks use double rings."],["P",{},"Sheep Heart virtual dissection. Specimen-specific procedure: Sheep heart chamber-window access; recommended view ventral; access route guided surface-to-chamber window; protected landmarks Coronary Arteries, Conduction System, Chordae Tendineae. Current view: ventral, standard condition, External layer. Living function model active: Coordinated cardiac cycle; this represents in-life physiology, not preserved specimen motion. Progressive exposure: Covered anatomy, 32 percent; decision quality 64 percent. Observation field: Limited observation field, 43 percent; limiting factor: Access width. Surface model: directional myocardial fibers. Surface microtexture is suppressed for visual clarity. Tool contact response: fiber compression. Light direction: overhead. Illumination 68 percent: Balanced illumination; glare risk 4 percent. Focus isolation on. Tissue relief on and responsive to the inspection light. Depth atlas on, with circle surface markers, diamond mid-depth markers, and double-ring deep markers. Selected and hovered structures use a labeled depth footprint with front and back edges, visibility-specific line styles, and boundary-attached relationship paths. Relationship types use distinct line patterns and moving marker shapes, with fixed markers when motion is reduced. Tubular, lobed, chambered, layered, and compact structure classes use distinct contours, internal patterns, and text labels. View and layer navigation uses a labeled orientation sweep with circle start and diamond target markers, plus concentric depth rings for deeper or superficial movement; reduced motion switches instantly and uses text announcements. Depth motion on. 7 directly visible structures. Active instrument: probe. Action readiness: Prediction required, 80 percent. Complete the prediction checkpoint before this instrument can contact the specimen.. Directional contact footprint feedback is active. Engaged instrument tips use foreground tissue lips and paired depth notches to show partial insertion and opening-edge occlusion. Guided anatomical access corridor active; circle marks start and diamond marks finish. Directional target guidance uses a dashed arrow, labeled endpoint, and shape-coded reticle when an instrument is spatially misaligned. Localized action outcomes use a one-shot check or warning symbol with the changed tissue metric. Tissue opening, retraction, stabilization, hydration, and risk metrics transition spatially after each action; Undo reverses openings, retraction, pin placement, hydration, wick evidence, and probe traces; reduced motion snaps to the final state. Three-dimensional instrument pitch feedback is active. Light-aware instrument elevation feedback is active. Equivalent keyboard and button actions include an instrument approach, contact, and release replay. Recorded attempt replay progressively traces pressure with line width and states path alignment in text and shape. Direct forceps press-drag-release manipulation shows live lift direction, safe range, calibrated grip, speed, control, slip risk, and excess tension with text and geometry; the technique action button remains equivalent. Live pin stability preview shows endpoint spacing, calibrated angle, insertion depth, and flap tension with text and geometry. Direct pin press-drag-release placement adds shaft alignment, insertion travel, safe depth, and control feedback. Live probe palpation preview shows calibrated pressure, material resistance, anatomical depth, and tissue deformation with text and shape. Direct probe press-drag-release tracing adds contact coverage, alignment, resistance, deformation, and control feedback. Direct dropper press-drag-release hydration adds contact coverage, flow alignment, moisture, dose, and pooling feedback. Direct wick press-drag-release recovery adds pool-edge alignment, contact coverage, recovery distance, and control feedback. Live dropper spread forecast shows dose count, organism-specific flow direction, current saturation, and pooling risk with text and geometry. Absorbent-wick feedback shows pool-edge targeting, fluid recovery, and a localized balanced-film marker with text and cross-hatched geometry. Guided procedure handoff cue connects the next required instrument to its anatomical target and 6-step progress rail with text, shape, and line style. Persistent localized technique evidence maps edge stress, grip compression, anchor tension, probe pressure, and saline pooling to actual contact locations with text and distinct geometry; direct wick recovery maps pool-edge contact, recovery distance, and control. Press a visible structure, trace a short path while monitoring pressure, resistance, and contact, then release when controlled; the equivalent technique action button remains available. Occluded structures remain available in the structure directory."]],["CANVAS",{"__playwright_bounding_rect__":"{\"left\":265,\"top\":1286.453125,\"right\":915,\"bottom\":2066.453125}","role":"application","id":"diss-canvas","data-diss-canvas":"true","data-cursor-mode":"explore","data-cursor-tone":"ready","aria-roledescription":"interactive specimen canvas","aria-labelledby":"diss-canvas-label","aria-keyshortcuts":"ArrowUp ArrowDown ArrowLeft ArrowRight Shift+ArrowUp Shift+ArrowDown Shift+ArrowLeft Shift+ArrowRight Home End Enter Space 0 R V X M P F 1 2 3 4 5 6 7","tabindex":"0","aria-describedby":"diss-canvas-status diss-canvas-equivalent","width":"650","height":"780","class":"diss-canvas w-full rounded-xl border border-slate-400 cursor-crosshair","style":"aspect-ratio: 5 / 6; background: var(--allo-stem-canvas, #0f172a); cursor: crosshair; touch-action: pan-y;","data-dissection-hud":"detailed","data-diss-compact-markers":"true"}]]],["DIV",{"class":"diss-marker-guide","data-diss-marker-guide":"true"},["P",{},"Numbered markers match the numbers in the structure list."],["BUTTON",{"type":"button","aria-controls":"diss-structure-directory"},"Browse structures"]],["DIV",{"class":"diss-zoom-bar flex items-center justify-center gap-2 py-1 px-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-100 border border-slate-400"},["BUTTON",{"aria-label":"Zoom out canvas","class":"transition-colors px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-bold bg-white border border-slate-400 hover:bg-slate-50 active:scale-[0.97]"},"➖"],["SPAN",{"class":"text-[11px] font-mono text-slate-600 min-w-[40px] text-center"},"100%"],["BUTTON",{"aria-label":"Zoom in canvas","class":"transition-colors px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs font-bold bg-white bor